course: dictionary
lesson: 647
title: Tiny virus hunters
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bacteriophage is a virus that attacks bacteria and kills them. Its short name is phage. Bacteria are tiny living things, so the word tells you a phage is aimed at bacteria.
explain: Read the context: «Bacteriophages are viruses that attack bacteria; they look like tiny moon landers, inject their instructions and burst the bacterium.» The words viruses and attack are clues. The sentence says the phage bursts the bacterium, so it does more than hurt it.
explain: Be careful with what the sentence does not say. Phages do not infect people, so they are not the germs that make you ill in the same way. Scientists study them, and doctors sometimes use them when antibiotics fail.
fact: A bacteriophage is a virus that infects and kills bacteria.
fact: Phage is the short name for bacteriophage.
fact: Bacteriophages is the plural, meaning more than one.
fact: Scientists study phages to fight bacteria that antibiotics cannot kill.
fact: Phages do not infect people.
exercise: «Bacteriophages are viruses that attack bacteria.» What do bacteriophages attack? | People's brains | *Bacteria | Plants only | Moon landers
exercise: What is another short name for bacteriophage? | Microbe lander | Bacterium | *Phage | Antibiotic
exercise: Why do scientists study phages? | *To find ways to fight bacteria that antibiotics cannot kill | To make bacteria stronger | Because phages are very large and easy to see | Because a phage can be a clue to a new kind of plant
test: Do bacteriophages infect people? | Yes, always | Sometimes, if you are ill | *No, they infect bacteria, not people | Yes, but only in summer
test: Which best describes a bacteriophage? | A tiny moon lander | A kind of bacteria that eats viruses | *A virus that infects and kills bacteria | A type of antibiotic medicine
exam: Using the clue in «Bacteriophages are viruses that attack bacteria», what kind of thing is a bacteriophage? | An antibiotic | A type of bacteria | A moon lander | *A virus
